WEBVTT

00:00.330 --> 00:04.794
A neighbourhood organiser notices that people who reply quickly in the group chat are

00:04.807 --> 00:06.606
more likely to attend events.

00:07.002 --> 00:11.020
She proposes removing anyone who has not replied within a day.

00:11.415 --> 00:14.170
Fast replies show commitment, she says.

00:14.539 --> 00:19.476
But a pattern in the records does not yet explain why people attend, or what a faster

00:19.488 --> 00:20.700
deadline would change.

00:21.747 --> 00:26.721
People with predictable working hours might find both replying and attending easier.

00:27.118 --> 00:31.314
Regular participants might check the chat because they already feel involved.

00:31.697 --> 00:36.212
Making everyone reply faster would not necessarily make attendance easier.

00:36.620 --> 00:40.970
The new rule might simply exclude people who cannot check their phones at work.

00:41.378 --> 00:46.620
These are possible explanations to investigate, not findings about every resident.

00:47.663 --> 00:49.602
Keep four questions separate.

00:50.012 --> 00:52.333
What happened asks for a clear record.

00:52.742 --> 00:57.168
What is likely next time asks whether a pattern also predicts later events,

00:57.436 --> 00:58.902
including its mistakes.

00:59.311 --> 01:02.295
Why did it happen asks which explanation fits.

01:02.678 --> 01:06.428
What should we do also involves consequences and fairness.

01:06.836 --> 01:11.007
The same attendance list cannot answer all four questions by itself.

01:12.038 --> 01:14.921
The organiser sees people who joined the chat.

01:15.318 --> 01:20.178
She misses residents who never received an invitation, could not use the platform, or

01:20.191 --> 01:22.180
left after an unwelcoming visit.

01:22.563 --> 01:25.356
Even a blank attendance box is ambiguous.

01:25.765 --> 01:29.413
Was the person absent, or did nobody record their arrival?

01:29.782 --> 01:33.264
Before judging someone, check what the record means and whose

01:33.277 --> 01:34.820
experience it leaves out.

01:35.872 --> 01:39.124
Now consider a separate invented screening example.

01:39.533 --> 01:42.990
Of one thousand people, ten deliberately broke a rule.

01:43.398 --> 01:47.735
A warning correctly flags eight of those ten, but also wrongly flags

01:47.748 --> 01:49.151
ninety-nine other people.

01:49.559 --> 01:54.457
That leaves one hundred and seven warnings, with only eight actual cases among them.

01:54.840 --> 01:58.717
Finding most cases does not mean that most warnings are correct.

01:59.747 --> 02:04.122
A base rate is how common something is in the relevant group before you consider a

02:04.134 --> 02:06.239
particular clue or test result.

02:06.623 --> 02:10.130
In that invented group, it is ten out of one thousand.

02:10.539 --> 02:14.442
The numbers demonstrate arithmetic, not a real screening tool.

02:14.824 --> 02:17.618
If the starting rate is unknown, say so.

02:18.000 --> 02:22.005
Do not invent a precise probability about a person, or dismiss

02:22.018 --> 02:23.574
specific evidence of harm.

02:24.622 --> 02:26.318
Return to the organiser.

02:26.727 --> 02:30.707
She learns that several slow responders work shifts and attend when

02:30.720 --> 02:32.059
given more time to book.

02:32.467 --> 02:34.827
That weakens her first explanation.

02:35.209 --> 02:38.041
It does not establish one reason for everyone.

02:38.436 --> 02:43.309
She can change the deadline and explain what information she needs, while recognising

02:43.322 --> 02:46.842
that an unanswered message does not measure how much someone cares.

02:47.872 --> 02:53.012
Julia Rohrer’s paper on investigating causes explains why things can happen together

02:53.025 --> 02:57.642
without one causing the other, and why causal conclusions depend on assumptions.

02:58.039 --> 03:02.529
For the organiser, that means checking alternatives before changing the rule.

03:02.924 --> 03:05.093
She may still need a booking deadline.

03:05.462 --> 03:09.748
Evidence can clarify the costs of different choices, but the group must

03:09.760 --> 03:11.380
also decide what is fair.

03:12.413 --> 03:15.998
When a pattern makes you want to change a rule, ask: What else

03:16.010 --> 03:17.362
could explain this pattern?

03:17.746 --> 03:21.777
Name what was actually observed, another possible explanation, and

03:21.789 --> 03:23.180
one fact you could check.

03:23.588 --> 03:27.402
Use more care when a mistaken judgment could cause serious harm.

03:27.810 --> 03:32.274
Then try the short practice section below the video with a different situation.
